    title: "The Limits of Informed Consent",
    extract: "Informed consent is often described as the cornerstone of medical ethics, yet the concept rests on assumptions about rational agency that clinical practice routinely undermines. The standard model holds that a competent patient, having received adequate information, voluntarily authorizes a medical procedure. Each element of this triad is more fragile than it appears. Competence is not a binary condition but a fluctuating capacity influenced by pain, fear, sedation, and cognitive load; a patient who can articulate a coherent preference in the morning may be incapable of doing so after hours in an emergency waiting room. The adequacy of information is similarly contested. Studies consistently show that patients retain only a fraction of what they are told during consent discussions, particularly when under stress. Physicians tend to overestimate how much has been understood, in part because patients nod and sign. The voluntariness requirement is perhaps the most philosophically demanding: genuine choice requires meaningful alternatives, yet patients who cannot afford the only treatment available, or who fear abandonment by their care team, are not exercising unfettered will. These structural pressures do not render informed consent worthless, but they do require that we reconceptualize it not as an event — a signed form — but as a continuing process embedded in a therapeutic relationship. The form documents an agreement; the relationship is what makes that agreement meaningful. Critics who argue that the current procedural apparatus actually serves institutional and legal interests more than patient autonomy are not entirely wrong. Stripping the process down to paperwork insulates providers from liability while leaving patients uninformed. A more honest accounting would acknowledge that consent is an ideal toward which clinicians strive, not a guarantee that any particular patient has genuinely understood and freely chosen. The implications for reform are significant: shorter, clearer disclosure documents; teach-back verification of understanding; longitudinal conversations rather than pre-procedural recitations; and attention to the power asymmetries inherent in the patient-physician relationship.",
