    title: "Paradigm Shifts and Scientific Progress",
    extract: "Thomas Kuhn's account of scientific revolutions remains one of the most provocative contributions to the philosophy of science, not because it resolved the question of how science progresses, but because it reframed what that question means. For Kuhn, normal science is not the heroic accumulation of truth but a puzzle-solving enterprise conducted within a framework — a paradigm — that defines which questions are legitimate and what counts as an answer. Scientists working within a paradigm are not open-minded truth-seekers in the romantic sense; they are highly skilled practitioners who have internalized a set of assumptions so deeply that those assumptions become invisible. Anomalies accumulate — findings that the paradigm cannot explain — but they are typically set aside, attributed to measurement error or experimental failure, rather than allowed to challenge foundational commitments. The transition from one paradigm to another is not a rational progression but a gestalt switch: a moment at which the old framework suddenly looks untenable and a new one snaps into focus. This is why Kuhn argued controversially that successive paradigms are incommensurable — there is no neutral language in which to compare the claims of Newtonian mechanics and relativistic physics, because each framework defines its own standards of evidence and explanation. Critics have rightly challenged the extremity of this claim. Scientists do manage to compare theories, evaluate evidence across frameworks, and make progress that is not purely arbitrary. But Kuhn's insight that the standards of scientific rationality are themselves products of historical communities, not timeless logical principles, remains indispensable. It means that science is a human institution, capable of both extraordinary achievement and persistent bias. The sociology of scientific knowledge that Kuhn inspired has been productive even when it overreached. The replication crisis afflicting contemporary biomedical and psychological research is, in one sense, a Kuhnian drama: a set of anomalies too large to dismiss forcing a reckoning with the methodological assumptions of an entire research culture.",
