    title: "On the Morality of Genetic Enhancement",
    extract: "The distinction between treatment and enhancement has anchored bioethical debate about genetic intervention since the first recombinant DNA experiments. Treating disease — restoring a person to normal functioning — is widely regarded as a permissible goal of medicine. Enhancing capacities beyond normal functioning raises more complex questions. But the distinction is less stable than it appears. Normal is a statistical concept, not a normative one: the average height or IQ of any population is the outcome of historical and environmental contingencies, not a morally privileged baseline. A child born with a genetic variant that produces mild intellectual disability is a candidate for treatment under standard bioethical reasoning. A child of average intelligence whose parents wish to give them a cognitive advantage is a candidate for enhancement. Yet the moral difference between these interventions depends entirely on what we take normal to mean and why we privilege it. Defenders of the treatment-enhancement distinction often invoke an appeal to nature: we should restore what has been lost or was never properly present, not manufacture new capacities. But this argument struggles with the fact that medicine routinely enhances: vaccines confer immunity beyond what the natural immune system would develop; corrective lenses exceed the visual acuity of the unaugmented eye; cognitive enhancers used by healthy medical students are socially tolerated. The more productive question is not whether enhancement as such is permissible but what constraints should govern it. Justice concerns are paramount: genetic enhancements available only to the wealthy will widen inequalities across generations in ways that dwarf existing educational or economic advantages. A just framework would need to either universalize access or demonstrate that permitting private enhancement does not worsen the situation of the least advantaged — a difficult case to make when the enhancement in question is cognitive capacity, which compounds over a lifetime and a career.",
