    title: "Cultural Relativism and Its Discontents",
    extract: "Cultural relativism — the position that moral norms are valid only relative to the cultures in which they arise, and that cross-cultural moral judgment is therefore a form of intellectual imperialism — has served important purposes in the history of anthropology and postcolonial thought. It was a necessary corrective to the arrogant ethnocentrism that accompanied European colonialism, and it remains a useful methodological principle: suspending judgment long enough to understand practices from the inside produces better ethnography and more honest cross-cultural dialogue. The problems arise when a methodological posture becomes a philosophical conclusion. As a description of how moral beliefs vary across cultures, cultural relativism is uncontroversial. As a normative claim — that no moral belief is more correct than any other across cultures — it is self-defeating. The claim that we ought not to impose our values on other cultures is itself a moral claim asserted universally; it cannot be justified by the relativist framework without contradiction. More practically, relativism provides no resources for criticizing practices that harm individuals within the cultures that sanction them: female genital cutting, honor violence, caste-based discrimination. The relativist must either condemn these practices on some standard beyond cultural consensus — in which case they have abandoned relativism — or remain silent, in which case they have abandoned the individuals harmed. The appropriate response to the failure of crude universalism is not relativism but a more humble and self-critical universalism. This means acknowledging that proposed universal norms have often reflected the interests and assumptions of the powerful, remaining genuinely open to revision in cross-cultural dialogue, and distinguishing between imposing values through political or economic coercion and defending values through argument and example. It does not mean pretending that no cross-cultural judgments can be justified.",
