    title: "Environmental Justice and the Distribution of Risk",
    extract: "The environmental justice movement emerged from a simple empirical observation: the burdens of environmental degradation — air pollution, toxic waste sites, industrial facilities — are distributed in the United States not by geography alone but by race and income. Communities of color and low-income communities bear a disproportionate share of environmental risk, not because they happen to live near industrial zones but because industrial zones tend to be located near them. The mechanisms of this distribution are multiple: land is cheaper in less powerful communities; zoning decisions reflect political power; existing residents lack the legal and financial resources to contest facility siting; and the cumulative logic of poverty means that any short-term economic benefit — jobs, local tax revenue — is more likely to be accepted by desperate communities than by affluent ones that have alternatives. The ethical implications extend beyond distributive justice in the conventional sense. Environmental harms are not static risks that happen to fall unevenly; they are produced by systems of extraction and production that generate wealth which accrues elsewhere. The community breathing petrochemical fumes does not share proportionally in the profits of the refinery it hosts. This constitutes not merely bad luck but a form of exploitation in which one community's health is converted into another community's economic gain. Proposed solutions range from procedural — ensuring meaningful community participation in siting decisions — to substantive — requiring that no community bear a disproportionate environmental burden regardless of whether it consents. The latter goes further and implies constraints on what democratic majorities can do to minorities, even when the majorities include the communities themselves trading health for economic necessity. That implication reveals that environmental justice is not merely a policy problem but a constitutional and philosophical one about the limits of consent under conditions of structural inequality.",
