    title: "The Myth of the Self-Made Individual",
    extract: "Few ideological commitments in American public culture are as durable as the idea of the self-made person: the individual who, through talent and effort alone, rises from modest origins to success. The narrative is compelling, and its appeal is not entirely without foundation — genuine social mobility exists, effort and talent matter, and the experiences of immigrants and working-class achievers deserve respect rather than condescension. But the narrative has a ideological function that goes beyond inspiration. By attributing success entirely to individual agency, it renders invisible the collective preconditions of any individual achievement: the infrastructure funded by taxes, the education system that formed the successful person, the social order maintained by institutions that enable contract enforcement and property rights, the family and community support that made sustained effort possible. The sociologist C. Wright Mills argued that personal troubles become public issues when their causes are structural, and that the mystification of structure as individual agency is a characteristic feature of ideological thought. The self-made narrative performs this mystification efficiently: it transforms what are in part structural advantages — access to networks, capital, education, and safety — into purely personal virtues, and structural disadvantages into purely personal failures. The political consequences are significant. If success is purely individual, then taxation of wealth is confiscatory rather than a return on collective investment. If failure is purely personal, then social support programs are charity to the undeserving rather than risk-pooling for a society in which individual outcomes are partly a function of luck and structural position. Neither the left-wing version of this argument — that individual agency is epiphenomenal — nor the right-wing version — that structural constraints are mere excuses — is adequate. The accurate picture requires holding individual responsibility and structural constraint simultaneously, which is intellectually demanding and politically inconvenient.",
