    title: "Standardized Testing and What It Measures",
    extract: "Standardized testing has become the dominant mechanism by which American educational institutions sort, rank, and select students, and the debate about its merits has grown correspondingly polarized. Defenders argue that standardized tests offer an objective, comparable measure of academic preparation that reduces the advantages of wealth, connections, and subjective impression in high-stakes decisions. Critics argue that standardized test scores reflect socioeconomic background more reliably than they reflect learning or potential, that the tests reward a narrow range of skills, and that teaching to the test has distorted educational priorities in ways that damage learning for students who will never attend selective institutions. Both sets of claims contain truth, and the debate is poorly served by treating them as mutually exclusive. Tests do provide some standardization across widely varying school quality and grade inflation. They also do correlate significantly with family income, and the causal pathways are at least partly structural: affluent families purchase test preparation, attend better-resourced schools, and can afford retakes. The key question is not whether tests measure something real — they demonstrably predict first-year college grades with modest accuracy — but whether they measure it well enough, and equitably enough, to justify their role in high-stakes decisions that shape life trajectories. The turn of some elite institutions away from standardized testing requirements has not eliminated these problems; holistic review introduces other forms of subjectivity and has not clearly increased socioeconomic diversity. What is missing from most policy debates is a clear account of what universities are actually trying to select for and whether any available instrument does so equitably. Without that clarity, debates about testing will continue to generate more heat than light.",
