    title: "The Political Economy of Drug Pricing",
    extract: "The price of pharmaceutical drugs in the United States is not set by the market in any meaningful sense of that term. It is the outcome of a negotiation between pharmaceutical manufacturers, pharmacy benefit managers, insurers, hospital systems, and, at the margins, government programs — a negotiation conducted in opacity, shaped by patent law, regulatory exclusivity, and the peculiar structure of American health insurance in which the buyer (the insurer), the payer (the employer or government), and the consumer (the patient) are different parties with different interests. The predictable result is that prices bear little relationship to development costs, production costs, or international comparators: the same insulin formulation sells for ten times more in the United States than in Canada, without any difference in the drug or the patients who need it. Defenders of the American system argue that high drug prices fund the research and development that produces medical innovation, and that countries with price controls free-ride on American R&D investment. This argument has some validity: pharmaceutical innovation is concentrated in countries with relatively high drug prices. But it proves less than its advocates claim. A substantial fraction of the basic research underlying major pharmaceutical innovations has been funded by public grants, primarily from the NIH, which means that American taxpayers pay twice — once for the research and once for the resulting drug at monopoly prices. Patent terms of twenty years, combined with regulatory exclusivity periods and strategic litigation to delay generic entry, extend effective market exclusivity far beyond what innovation cost recovery would require. The political economy of drug pricing is also a story about lobbying: the pharmaceutical industry is consistently among the largest spenders on federal lobbying and campaign contributions, and the legislative failures to enable Medicare drug price negotiation for over two decades reflect political influence as much as economic argument.",
